+
+## TL;DR
+- **Best legal knowledge base:** [OpenContracts](https://github.com/Open-Source-Legal/OpenContracts) for self-hosted legal document annotation, search, extraction, and AI-agent workflows.
+- **Best open case-law data platform:** [CourtListener](https://github.com/freelawproject/courtlistener) for searchable opinions, oral arguments, dockets, judges, and filings.
+- **Best contract and legal text NLP library:** [LexNLP](https://github.com/LexPredict/lexpredict-lexnlp) for clause extraction, legal entities, dates, citations, and numeric parsing.
+- **Best OCR stack for legal PDFs:** [PaddleOCR](https://github.com/PaddlePaddle/PaddleOCR) plus [OCRmyPDF](https://github.com/ocrmypdf/OCRmyPDF) for scanned pleadings, exhibits, and discovery.
+- **Best privacy-first local workspace:** [AnythingLLM](https://github.com/Mintplex-Labs/anything-llm) or [Open WebUI](https://github.com/open-webui/open-webui) for private document chat on local or self-hosted models.
+
+## Why This List
+Law firms and in-house teams handle contracts, pleadings, discovery, policies, invoices, transcripts, and privileged work product. For that reason, the best AI tools for lawyers 2026 are not just “smart”; they also need auditable code, controllable deployments, and clear licenses. This list only includes public GitHub repositories, excludes proprietary SaaS products, and favors tools that help with legal research, contract analysis, OCR, redaction, document intelligence, and confidential local AI workflows.
+
+## Open Source Tools
+
+### Legal Research, Case Law, and Legal NLP
+
+#### [OpenContracts](https://github.com/Open-Source-Legal/OpenContracts)
+> **Description:** OpenContracts is a self-hosted platform for building legal and regulatory knowledge bases from source documents. Its README emphasizes human annotation as the ground truth, then layers AI agents, semantic search, threaded collaboration, structured extraction, and PDF layout fidelity on top. That makes it unusually useful for contract review teams, litigators organizing exhibits, and legal ops groups building reusable clause libraries. It is licensed under AGPL-3.0 and is designed for teams that want to keep documents in their own environment while still using modern retrieval and extraction workflows. GitHub shows strong community traction and active maintenance.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/Open-Source-Legal/OpenContracts](https://github.com/Open-Source-Legal/OpenContracts)
+- **Stars:** 1.3k ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** AGPL-3.0
+- **Last Commit:** Feb 8, 2026
+- **Category:** legal-knowledge-base, annotation, document-ai
+- **Best for:** Self-hosted legal document review, annotation, and extraction
+
+---
+
+#### [CourtListener](https://github.com/freelawproject/courtlistener)
+> **Description:** CourtListener is the Free Law Project’s open archive of case-law and court data, covering opinions, oral arguments, judges, financial disclosures, and federal filings. The project README and repository description center on searchable public legal information at scale, which makes it valuable for litigators, researchers, clerks, and legal data teams building precedent or docket-monitoring workflows. It is one of the strongest GitHub-native legal data projects in public view. The codebase is Python-based, actively updated, and appropriate for teams that need transparent infrastructure around legal search instead of relying entirely on closed research platforms.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/freelawproject/courtlistener](https://github.com/freelawproject/courtlistener)
+- **Stars:** 858 ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** BSD-2-Clause
+- **Last Commit:** Feb 27, 2026
