+## FAQ
+
+### What are the best open source AI tools for lawyers in 2026?
+For most teams, the strongest starting set is OpenContracts for legal knowledge bases, CourtListener for public legal data, LexNLP for legal text extraction, PaddleOCR plus OCRmyPDF for scanned PDFs, and AnythingLLM or Open WebUI for private document chat.
+
+### Are there truly free AI tools for lawyers on GitHub?
+Yes, but “free” depends on license and infrastructure. Every tool in this list has a public GitHub repository. Some are permissive, such as MIT or Apache-2.0. Others use AGPL or GPL licenses, which still qualify as open source but come with stronger obligations.
+
+### Can law firms self-host these tools to protect client confidentiality?
+Usually yes. That is one reason these repositories were chosen. Tools like OpenContracts, DocuSeal, OpenSign, AnythingLLM, Open WebUI, RAGFlow, PaddleOCR, and OCRmyPDF are especially useful when firms want private deployments around sensitive documents.
+
+### Which open source AI tool is best for contract review?
+There is no single winner. For contract extraction and knowledge building, OpenContracts, LexNLP, and ContextGem are the most directly relevant. For turning scans into analyzable text first, use PaddleOCR, OCRmyPDF, or MinerU.
+
+### Which GitHub repos are best for legal research and case-law search?
+CourtListener is the strongest direct legal research repository in this list. Blackstone and LexGLUE are more useful for teams building legal-domain NLP or evaluation systems on top of case-law and legal text corpora.
+
+### Are there enough pure open source legal AI repos to replace closed legal SaaS?
+Not yet. The category is still thin compared with general AI infrastructure. That is why this list includes adjacent but lawyer-relevant tools for OCR, redaction, document parsing, privacy, and local RAG, instead of pretending GitHub already has 50 polished legal-native products.
+
+### What should lawyers avoid when choosing an AI GitHub tool?
+Avoid confusing a flashy demo with a usable legal workflow. Check license terms, maintenance pace, PDF handling quality, privacy posture, and whether the repo can actually be self-hosted with your document corpus. Closed APIs wrapped in a thin UI are not enough for serious legal work.
+
+## GitHub Search Queries Used
+
+The following reproducible GitHub CLI queries were used to widen the search beyond the exact phrase “AI tools for lawyers,” which returned few direct matches:
+
+```bash
+gh search repos "topic:legaltech" --limit 50 --sort stars
+gh search repos "topic:legaltech contracts" --limit 50 --sort updated
